sections.body:
The page describes violations in prose; code shows why they hurt. The code below was run and its output checked.

LSP violation:

class Bird:
    def fly(self): return 'flying'
class Penguin(Bird):
    def fly(self): raise NotImplementedError('penguins cannot fly')
def migrate(birds): return [b.fly() for b in birds]
migrate([Bird(), Penguin()])   # raises NotImplementedError

The caller was written against Bird and is now broken by a subclass. The fix is the page's: do not promise fly() on the base. Give both birds a common move() and let only the flying kind fly.

OCP and DIP together:

class Card:
    def pay(self, cents): return f'card:{cents}'
class EFT:
    def pay(self, cents): return f'eft:{cents}'
def checkout(method, cents): return method.pay(cents)

A new method (EFT) is added without editing checkout, which depends on the abstraction 'something with pay()', not on any concrete method. Running checkout over Card, PayPal and EFT objects returned card:1000, paypal:1000 and eft:1000.
